@Francis

I've been a baseball fan all my life but only recently learned that if the batter is going for a double/triple but is thrown out at second/third, he is credited with a single/double (i.e., the last base he crossed safely).

In a recent Dodgers game, LF Hernandez hit a ball that got by a diving CF and went to the wall. Hernandez tried for an in-the-park homer but was thrown out at home. Nevertheless, he's credited with a triple.

@Mean Old Lady

Arg.  You and my wife.  We've had the conversation dozens of times, concerning no-hitters and perfect games.  

She's adamant that anytime the ball touches the bat, it's a hit.

@Francis
Glad you two seem to have learned to remain agreeable while disagreeing. After all, you're still married, right?
Now if only you could teach the rest of us...

@Bill in Yokohama
And in that Hernandez play, I'd like to point out that it was the Cincinnati Reds' shortstop, Elly de la Cruz, who threw the ball from shallow center field (at 102 mph!) to  catch Hernandez at the plate. 

The Reds are going nowhere and the Dodgers are going to the playoffs, so we Cincinnati fans are really glad we at least have Elly. (And Sal Stewart is another success story; they're few and far between this year.)

@RL
I love foreign-language clues! I've never had a minute of formal Spanish but between French classes, US television, and many occasions to hear Spanish spoken in person, somehow it's oozed into my brain.
I even love Asian-language clues. I have pretty well zero vocabulary or experience with anything west of China and east of Iran, but I love the challenge.

@G

Close enough.  Google says it’s 24 minutes from me.  But I didn’t even know there was a petting zoo in Ramapo. 

It’s funny what you learn from crosswords even when it’s not actually in the crossword.

@Steve L

Yes, and it's funny what makes you think of crosswords – and the crossword community – even when you're not in the middle of solving one. 

Hopefully you'll have reason to put your newfound kiddie entertainment knowledge to use! :)

@G

I doubt I'll be putting this knowledge to practical use, as our kids are in their 30s and they don't have any kids, either.  

This petting zoo, FWIW, is apparently run by (and I assume aimed mostly at) Orthodox Jews, because of the location, the normal hours (closed Saturday) and the special closures noted on their website (the various non-Saturday Jewish holidays this time of year).

